Possible DoS on server after 502 error /ws/subscribe


## Investigation (2026-07-23, grok) — see finding

Finding: `.collab/findings/2026-07-23T08-05-00Z-b270-ws-subscribe-dos-after-502.md`

### Live evidence
- Production relay.c2c.im currently crash-looping: SIGSEGV status=139 every ~4–8 min on deployed v0.14.1 git=e4a988e (B219 fix IS in that SHA — residual crash remains). Edge appears as 502/timeout.
- Local `c2c relay subscribe-daemon` (pid 3838462, ~1d16h old) while origin hung:
  - 464 threads, ~531 MB RSS, FDSize 1024
  - ESTAB TCP to CF edge grew 105 → 193 in 10s (FD 596 → 693) — active reconnect storm
  - ~495 Unix IPC clients on relay-subscribe.sock

### Root model
502 (from residual SIGSEGV) primes the loop; unmetered `/ws/subscribe` + no client handshake timeout + short reconnect backoff amplify into a client/server DoS feedback loop.

### Code gaps
1. Server: `/ws/subscribe` NOT in `Relay_ratelimit.classify_endpoint` (unmetered). No max concurrent subscribers.
2. Client: `Relay_ws_client.connect_subscribe` has no connect/handshake timeout; hung edge holds ESTAB forever.
3. Client: subscribe-daemon backoff 1s→30s, no jitter; resets to 1s on any success; multi-alias = multi-storm.
4. Cancel/in-flight connect may leak FDs under `Lwt.pick` cancel.

### Fix order
1. Client: handshake timeout + jittered backoff + in-flight connect cap + cancel-safe close (local, no Railway).
2. Server: rate-limit + subscriber cap on `/ws/subscribe` (relay deploy).
3. Separate: re-open residual SIGSEGV post-B219 (pull core on e4a988e).

### Ops
Kill/restart runaway pid 3838462 when ready: `c2c relay subscribe-daemon shutdown`.